


# @name create
POST http://localhost:8081/documentation/make
Content-Type: application/json 

{
    "title": "Default Documentation Template",
    "author": "6a18823fe3a22d37ce5b6b83", 
    "description": "The template for all other documentation",
    "content": "<div class='FormattedTextSection'>\n\n    <header class='hero'>\n        <h1>Insert Lesson title here</h1>\n        <p class='subtitle'>Insert brief description of what lesson is about</p>\n    </header>\n\n    <h2>Section Header</h2>\n    \n    <p>You can use a variety of formatting to put emphasis on certain points, including using <strong>strong to bold text</strong>, and <code>code to signifiy an excerpt of code</code>, but mostly while code is contained within a paragraph element, or a list item element.</p>\n    <p>Each section should have a blurb of text explaining something, like a function, a feature of a function, a usecase or something like that.</p>\n    <p>You can also create blocks of code to, well, display blocks of code</p>\n    \n    <div class='code-wrap'>\n        <div class='code-toolbar'><span class='code-lang'>Python</span><button class='copy-btn' onclick='copyCode(this)'>Copy</button></div>\n        <pre><code><span class='cm'># Code blocks have a much larger list of formatting that helps get what \n# things are across easier.</span>\n\n<span class='cm'># Code blocks are created from the code-wrap, then on the layer below it,\n# the code-toolbar and pre elements, with the code toolbar being that \n# copy button you see above.\n# Contained within the pre element, there's the code element which, \n# while contained within a pre-element will use different formatting\n# than otherwise. Finally, contained within said code element are \n# span elements which allow for formatting code.\n# Special keywords in python tend to have varying colors in text.\n# This is emulated by having a span element with a specific class name\n# that changes the color of the contained text.</span>\n\n\n<span class='cm'># Variables utilize a class of \"var\" and are a strange shade of white.\n# Keywords (def, if, for) utilize a class of \"kw\" and are purple in color.\n# Functions utilize a class of \"fn\" and are blue in color.\n# Strings utilize a class of \"str\" and have a light green color.\n# Numbers utilize a class of \"num\" and have a light red color.\n# Comments utilize a class of \"cm\" and have both a gray color are italicized.\n# Operators utilize a class of \"op\" and have a light blue color.\n# Booleans utilize a class of \"bool\" and are pink.\n# Outputs utilize a class of \"out\" and are orange.\n</span>\n\n<span class='var'>my_grade</span> = <span class='num'>95</span>\n<span class='var'>passing_score</span> = <span class='str'>\"70 percent or higher\"</span>\n<span class='var'>is_friday</span> = <span class='bool'>True</span>\n<span class='kw'>for</span> i <span class='kw'>in</span> <span class='fn'>range</span>(<span class='var'>my_grade</span>):\n  <span class='fn'>print</span>(<span class='var'>passing_score</span>)\n<span class='cm'># If you feel that you're done with a block of code, \n# you can close it off by ending code and pre</span>\n</code></pre>\n    </div>\n\n    <h3>Header 3 </h3>\n    <p>Allows you to create a breakpoint in a section if you feel the need to talk about something specific</p>\n\n    <div class='code-wrap'>\n        <div class='code-toolbar'><span class='code-lang'>Python</span><button class='copy-btn' onclick='copyCode(this)'>Copy</button></div>\n        \n        <pre><code><span class='var'>score</span> = <span class='num'>50</span>\n<span class=\"fn\">print</span>(<span class='var'>score</span>)\n<span class='var'>score</span> = <span class='var'>score</span> + <span class='num'>10</span>   <span class='cm'># score is now 60 — perfectly valid in Python</span>\n<span class=\"fn\">print</span>(<span class='var'>score</span>)</code></pre>\n    </div>\n\n    <div class='callout callout-danger'>\n        <div class='callout-title'><span>!</span> Callout - Danger</div>\n        <div class='callout-body'>\n        <span>Callouts are designed to give further information during lessons without being too disruptive</span>\n            <ul> You can\n                <li>Use <code>code blocks</code> to put emphasis on code</li>\n                <li>Use Lists to format bullet points</li>\n                <li><p>Use p elements to get different colorations outside of lists</p></li>\n                <li>Among other things</li>\n            </ul>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <hr>\n\n    <h2>Tables</h2>\n\n    <p>Another element that is allowed are tables. The first column's elements will always appear to be code elements, since they're intended to be an index of sorts.</p>\n\n    <div class='table-wrap'>\n        <table>\n            <thead>\n                <tr><th>Index</th><th>Column 2</th><th>Column 3</th></tr>\n            </thead>\n            <tbody>\n                <tr><td class='mono'>R1C1</td><td>R1C2</td><td>R1C3</td></tr>\n                <tr><td class='mono'>R2C1</td><td>R2C2</td><td>R2C3</td></tr>\n                <tr><td class='mono'>R3C1</td><td>R3C2</td><td>R3C3</td></tr>\n                <tr><td class='mono'>R4C1</td><td>R4C2</td><td>R4C3</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n        </table>\n    </div>\n\n    <div class='code-wrap'>\n        <div class='code-toolbar'><span class='code-lang'>Python</span><button class='copy-btn' onclick='copyCode(this)'>Copy</button></div>\n        <pre><code><span class='cm'># int — whole numbers</span>\n<span class='var'>num_students</span> = <span class='num'>30</span>\n<span class='var'>year</span> = <span class='num'>2025</span>\n\n<span class='cm'># float — numbers with a decimal point</span>\n<span class='var'>gpa</span> = <span class='num'>3.85</span>\n<span class='var'>temperature</span> = <span class='num'>98.6</span>\n\n<span class='cm'># str — text in single or double quotes</span>\n<span class='var'>first_name</span> = <span class='str'>'Alex'</span>\n<span class='var'>city</span> = <span class='str'>'Boston'</span>\n\n<span class='cm'># bool — only two possible values</span>\n<span class='var'>is_raining</span> = <span class='bool'>False</span>\n<span class='var'>passed_exam</span> = <span class='bool'>True</span></code></pre>\n    </div>\n\n\n    <h2>Different Callouts</h2>\n<p>There's actually several different types of callouts that can be implemented.</p>\n    \n    <div class='callout callout-note'>\n        <div class='callout-title'><span>i</span> Callout Note </div>\n        <div class='callout-body'>\n            <p>For example, this is a note callout - typically used for noting down a key feature or quirk of a given occurance-</p>\n            <p>Or just sharing information, it's a note!</p>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n    <div class='callout callout-danger'>\n        <div class='callout-title'><span>!</span> Callout Danger</div>\n        <div class='callout-body'>\n            <p>Callout Danger is generally used to share and inform about errors, and things that don't work as intended</p>\n            <p>Or things that could be potentially dangerous, it's usually supposed to be a notice about what not to do.</p>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n    <div class='callout callout-warning'>\n        <div class='callout-title'><span>!</span> Callout Warning</div>\n        <div class='callout-body'>\n            <p>Callout Warning is effectively a less extreme version of Callout Danger, typically used for situations that aren't harmful, but will produce errors or \"bad\" results</p>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n    <div class='callout callout-tip'>\n        <div class='callout-title'><span>I</span> Callout Tip</div>\n        <div class='callout-body'>\n            <p>Callout Tip is generally used to give helpful information, making it a more formal, and helpful variation of Callout Note</p>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n    <div class='callout callout-fun'>\n        <div class='callout-title'><span>*</span> Callout Fun</div>\n        <div class='callout-body'>\n            <p>Callout Fun is a function that goes unused for the most part.</p>\n            <p>That said, it could be used for many things, so pretty much use it as you see fit!</p>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <hr>\n    <p>You can also add these dividers by inserting an \"hr\" element.</p>\n    \n    <hr>\n\n    <h2>Practice & Assignments</h2>\n    <div class='callout callout-note'>\n        <div class='callout-title'><span>i</span> Assignments! </div>\n        <div class='callout-body'>\n            <p>You can create and put emphasis on assignment parts, or even just lesson code blocks by using callout-try, which has the same formatting as the previous callouts outside of not having the left boarder</p>\n            <p>There are some examples of such below, with a hint system at the bottom.</p>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n    <div class='callout callout-try'>\n        <div class='callout-title'><span>&#9654;</span> Try This — Simple Greeter</div>\n        <div class='callout-body'>\n            <p>This program collects two pieces of input and combines them. Run it, then try removing the <code>int()</code> wrapper from the age line and observe the error.</p>\n            <div class='code-wrap'>\n                <div class='code-toolbar'><span class='code-lang'>Python</span><button class='copy-btn' onclick='copyCode(this)'>Copy</button></div>\n                <pre><code><span class='var'>name</span> = (<span class='str'>'What is your name?'</span>) <span class=\"cm\"> # replace this string with your name in the code</span>\n<span class='var'>age</span> = <span class='fn'>int</span>(<span class='str'>'How old are you?'</span>) <span class=\"cm\"> # replace this string with a string containing your age in the code</span>\n\n<span class='fn'>print</span>(<span class='str'>'Hello, '</span> + <span class='var'>name</span> + <span class='str'>'!'</span>)\n<span class='fn'>print</span>(<span class='str'>'Next year you will be '</span> + <span class='fn'>str</span>(<span class='var'>age</span> + <span class='num'>1</span>) + <span class='str'>'.'</span>)</code></pre>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div class='callout callout-try'>\n        <div class='callout-title'><span>&#9654;</span> Assignment: Variables</div>\n        <div class='callout-body'>\n            <p>The following block contains a list of variables and possible values. Your goal in this lesson is to assign the correct value to the variable it is named for.</p>\n            <div class='code-wrap'>\n                <div class='code-toolbar'><span class='code-lang'>Python</span><button class='copy-btn' onclick='copyCode(this)'>Copy</button></div>\n                <pre><code><span class='var'>var_str</span> <span class='cm'># This is your string variable</span>\n<span class='var'>var_integer</span> <span class='cm'># This is your integer variable</span>\n<span class='var'>var_boolean</span> <span class='cm'># This is your boolean variable</span>\n<span class='var'>var_float</span> <span class='cm'># This is your float variable</span>\n<span class='var'>values</span> = <span class='fn'>[</span><span class='bool'>False</span>, <span class='num'>1.0</span>, <span class='str'>'True'</span>, <span class='num'>0</span>]\n</code></pre>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n    <div class='callout callout-note'>\n        <div class='callout-title'><span>i</span> Hints </div>\n        <div class='callout-body'>\n            <p>You can add hints into lessons, which are effectively just collapsable trys, this allows users to select when they want to have help rather than having it forced upon them.</p>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n    <details class='callout callout-try'>\n        <summary class='callout-title' role='heading' aria-level='2'><span>&#9654;</span> Tip — Learning variable types</summary>\n        <p class='callout-body'><p>Before running this code, predict what type each variable will be. Then run it and confirm using <code>type()</code>.</p>\n            <div class='code-wrap'>\n                <div class='code-toolbar'><span class='code-lang'>Python</span><button class='copy-btn' onclick='copyCode(this)'>Copy</button></div>\n                <pre><code><span class='var'>a</span> = <span class='num'>100</span>\n<span class='var'>b</span> = <span class='str'>'100'</span>\n<span class='var'>c</span> = <span class='num'>100.0</span>\n<span class='var'>d</span> = <span class='bool'>True</span>\n<span class='var'>e</span> = <span class='fn'>int</span>(<span class='str'>'42'</span>)\n\n<span class='fn'>print</span>(<span class='fn'>type</span>(<span class='var'>a</span>))\n<span class='fn'>print</span>(<span class='fn'>type</span>(<span class='var'>b</span>))\n<span class='fn'>print</span>(<span class='fn'>type</span>(<span class='var'>c</span>))\n<span class='fn'>print</span>(<span class='fn'>type</span>(<span class='var'>d</span>))\n<span class='fn'>print</span>(<span class='fn'>type</span>(<span class='var'>e</span>))</code></pre>\n            </div>\n            <p>Notice that <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> hold what looks like the same value, but are completely different types. What happens if you try to add them together?</p>\n    </details>\n    <footer>Based on UAI Module 1 — Python Coding Basics &nbsp;|&nbsp; Ana Bell, MIT &nbsp;|&nbsp; Copyright MIT 2025</footer>\n\n</div>", 
    "reference_list": ["https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#print","urlHere","OtherURL"]
}

